(Bug #81: the format-hole scanner stopped at the first `}` it met, blind to
 any quoted string inside the hole. A map read with a dynamic key
 (LANGUAGE.md:2400) inside a hole (LANGUAGE.md:3147) therefore ended halfway
 through the key, and the leftover `"` and `}` — the hole's own punctuation —
 spilled into the output with no error. A key holding a brace was read as the
 empty key and quietly returned the wrong value.)

a map called scores is {"ada": 10, "grace": 20}.
a text called player is "ada".

(The dynamic key. The hole ends at its own brace, not at the key's.)
Print "[{scores's \"{player}\"}]".

(The static key, which was always right, still is.)
Print "[{scores's \"grace\"}]".

(Text either side of the hole stays where it was written.)
Print "score for {player} is [{scores's \"{player}\"}] today".

(Two dynamic keys in one string, each ending at its own brace.)
a text called other is "grace".
Print "[{scores's \"{player}\"}] and [{scores's \"{other}\"}]".

(A key that contains the hole's own punctuation is still just a key.)
a map called punctuated is {"}": 1, "a}b": 3}.
Print "close: {punctuated's \"}\"}".
Print "both: {punctuated's \"a}b\"}".

(The nearest working neighbour: read the key out first. Unchanged.)
a number called got is scores's "{player}".
Print "[{got}]".
